Currently, when running {{processCcdWithFakes.py}}, the task will fail with a {{RuntimeError}} if no fake sources exist for a given dataRef. For example, running this command:
{code:sh}
$ processCcdWithFakes.py INPUT --output OUTPUT --id visit=1228 ccd=5 tract=9813 --config insertFakes.fakeType=FAKEFILE
{code}
will fail with this error:
{code:sh}
processCcdWithFakes FATAL: Failed on dataId={'visit': 1228, 'ccd': 5, 'tract': 9813, 'pointing': 817, 'filter': 'HSC-I'}: RuntimeError: No fakes found for this dataRef.
{code}
if no input fake source exists within CCD 5 of visit 1228.

It would be beneficial for {{processCcdWithFakes.py}} to have the option to generate a {{fakes_calexp}} and a {{fakes_src}} for all requested CCDs, regardless of whether or not each CCD actually contains an injected fake source. This would allow for subsequent coadd processing to generate equivalent coadd imaging using all the same processed input visits as a standard non-fake run. This ticket aims to add that functionality.

The coadd level catalogs include a flag:
{code:python}
"detect_isPrimary", type="Flag",
 doc="true if source has no children and is in the inner region of a coadd patch "
          "and is in the inner region of a coadd tract "
          "and is not \"detected\" in a pseudo-filter (see config.pseudoFilterList)",
{code}
that is very useful for culling catalogs of duplicate (patch/tract overlap), non-deblended parent sources ({{detect_nChild>0}}, and sky objects.  No equivalent flag currently exists at the visit /single frame measurement level, so this ticket is to add such a flag.  Currently, the only two conditions to be added to flag undesirable sources are:
{{detect_nChild > 0}}
{{sky_source is True}}

Ticket DM-20070 adds code to {cp_pipe/ptc.py} which is based on code by Pierre Astier. This code, in turn, implements a model of the PTC that is described in equation 20 of Astier+19. This ticket should compare the gains obtained by fitting this model ({ptcFitType = ASTIERFULL} in {ptc.py}) to the gains obtained by fitting the PTC to the existing {ASTIERAPPROXIMATION} (equation 16 in Astier+19) and {POLYNOMIAL} {ptcFitType} options.  

Potentially useful input data for this: 
* BOT data in {project/shared/BOT}
* auxTel data in {project/shared/auxTel}

