The way we wrap custom exceptions with pybind11 is a bit confusing and also needlessly difficult from packages outside of pex_exceptions.

The confusion is caused by using the same name for a C++ wrapper of the custom exception and the Python class that is actually used for that exception. Both are imported into the package namespace, but the Python version shadows the C++ version. This makes the code very confusing to read, and will cause problems if a user forgets to wrap the C++ version.

My proposed solution to that confusion is to name the C++ wrapper with a leading underscore, e.g. {{_RuntimeError}}. That is a clue to the user that the class should not be used in Python.

As to the difficulty of use outside of pex_exceptions, I suggest that the {{register}} function be imported into the {{lsst.pex.exceptions}} namespace and possibly renamed to {{registerException}} in order to reduce ambiguity. Thus this:
{code}
import lsst.pex.exceptions
import lsst.pex.exceptions.wrappers
...
@lsst.pex.exceptions.wrappers.register
class SinglePolygonException(lsst.pex.exceptions.RuntimeError):
    WrappedClass = _SinglePolygonException
{code}
can become this (without the ambiguity of importing {{register}} in the above):
{code}
import lsst.pex.exceptions
...
@lsst.pex.exceptions.registerException
class SinglePolygonException(lsst.pex.exceptions.RuntimeError):
    WrappedClass = _SinglePolygonException
{code}
I admit that the rename would be much more useful if we didn't have to use the pex_exceptions namespace to get to exception classes such as {{pex.exceptions.RuntimeError}} that collide with global exception names.

