Implement {{Ti.UI.WebView.release()}} to release memory when the web view is no longer needed. By using {{release()}} you don't need to wait for garbage collector to release the memory that WebView is using as browser component tends to use a lot of memory. 

https://docs.appcelerator.com/platform/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.WebView

{code}
var webview = Ti.UI.createWebView();
webview.release();
{code}

This is useful especially in our unit tests; our test suite creates WebView instances for each unit tests and {{release()}} gives us a way to release the WebView explicitly.

https://github.com/appcelerator/titanium_mobile_windows/pull/1378/files#diff-ddfcb7df7ea83b662ee76081f3ab07c4R59

We should make sure to catch every exception that is happening within async operations. We should catch {{Platform::COMException}} rather than {{Platform::Exception}} because WinRT tends to throw COMException which is _not_ a subclass of Platform::Exception.

Typically it's going to look like below.

{code}
concurrency::create_task(object__->DoSomethingAsync()).then([](concurrency::task<void> task)({
    try {
        task.get();
    } catch (Platform::COMException^ e) {
        // we might want to log error message, but
        // we should not throw exception inside async operations.
    } catch (...) {
        // we might want to log this, but
        // we should not throw exception inside async operations.
    }
})
{code}

We should make sure all JavaScript callback is done on UI thread. Otherwise we saw crash when callback touches UI properties especially on Windows Store apps. We haven't usually see this problem on Windows Phone apps.

Typically we use {{TitaniumWindows::Utility::RunOnUIThread}}. It's going to look like below:

{code}
concurrency::create_task(object__->DoSomethingAsync()).then([]()({
    TitaniumWindows::Utility::RunOnUIThread([](){
        js_callback();
    });
})
{code}

